  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Using Machine Learning to Reduce HIV Treatment Interruptions</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Approximately 39 million people were living with HIV/AIDS worldwide at the end of 2022. While great strides have been made in increasing access to treatment, interruption in treatment remains a persistent problem that can result in viral rebound, disease advancement, and premature mortality. A study in Akwa Ibom, Nigeria found the treatment retention rate for people living with HIV/AIDS to be just 68 percent after one year, significantly lower than the benchmark of 98 percent set by the Presidents Emergency Plan for AIDS Relief (PEPFAR) in Nigeria. To increase the treatment retention rate, FHI 360 developed and piloted a machine learning algorithm to identify people likely to interrupt treatment using patient data available through HIV/AIDS programs. Once the algorithm identifies people likely to interrupt treatment, providers can offer tailored care to address barriers that may prevent them from adhering to treatment. With support from Development Innovation Ventures and PEPFAR, FHI 360 will scale its program to 16,000 people across two Nigerian states and will conduct a randomized controlled trial to rigorously evaluate the effect of the program on measures of treatment interruption (e.g. missed appointments), patient viral loads, and patient satisfaction. This study will inform whether this machine learning approach can improve resource allocation and health outcomes and should ultimately be integrated into broader HIV/AIDS programming.</narrative>
  </description>
